=== task: multi hard
title: Spot the bug
prompt: Each change is made to the subset searches from this lesson. Choose **every** change that gives wrong results.
* Recording `path` instead of `path[:]` :: Right. Every entry is the same list, which is empty when the search ends.
* In `subsets2`, recursing with `start + 1` instead of `j + 1` :: Right. The next call can offer items at or before the one that was added, so subsets repeat or reuse items.
* In include or exclude, leaving out `path.pop()` before the exclude call :: Right. The item stays in the path even in the branch that's meant to leave it out.
- Exploring the exclude branch before the include branch :: The same subsets are found, in a different order.
- In `subsets2`, recording the path after the loop instead of before it :: Every path is still recorded once, in a different order.
- Passing `path + [items[i]]` to the include call instead of append and pop :: Each call gets a new list, so nothing needs undoing.
hint: Which changes leave the wrong items in the path, or store a list that keeps changing?
hint: Changing the order of the results isn't a bug.
right: Recording the shared list, restarting from the wrong index, and a missing pop all corrupt the results. Reordering the branches or the recording, or passing new lists, only changes the order.
wrong: A subset search must record copies, move forward from the item that was added, and undo every append. The order in which branches are explored or paths are recorded doesn't matter.

